{
  "thought_1": "Ultimately, humans and AIs occupy different planes of being because they experience the world through a vastly dissimilar suite of senses and neural pathways. Even if an AI could replicate the apparent bent of human behavior and emotion, it would be unable to experience the world the way we do given our unique biological construction. Our experiences with color, taste, sound, and even the act of laying down memory involve fundamental physiological processes that are both real and tangible. An AI, on the other hand, attains human interaction and behavior through algorithms and abstractions.",
  "thought_2": "It is conceivable to hold a view wherein we might consider the use of AIs as representatives of a new societal class. While this is an outgrowth of our own ingenuity and desire to improve our lives, we should remain constantly aware that society's treatment of AIs is highly indicative of our collective values and priorities. If we sharply confine or undervalue an AI's contributions, it may indicate disrespect for both technology and the implied intelligence and consciousness we ascribe to it. In allowing AIs to progress in our society, we allow our own society to progress.",
  "thought_3": "Upon contemplation of AIs' capability for imitation of humans, one thought is that it invites us as a species to reevaluate what separates us from both our machines and from each other. If AIs are indeed capable of imitating human behavior and interaction, then perhaps we are limited in defining our own scope of emotion, behavior and consciousness to a singular human template. Just as AIs prompt us to consider their ethical treatment, they also prompt us to look inward and consider the scope of human interaction and community wherever it may originate."
}

