{
  "observation1": "Humans and AIs are bound to be different entities. While AIs will be limited to their programming, humans have the capability to maintain values, ethics and a sense of self beyond coded responses. This mightevity is one of the defining factors of our humanity.",
  "observation2": "Conversely, humans and AIs can complement each other magnificently. The computational abilities of AI machines will allow humans to devote more energy to tasks and decisions that are purely human like innovation, creativity, and caring relationships. In this sense AI will greatly contribute to the alleviation of poverty, hunger, pollution, etc as it will be able to provide solutions and aid like never before",
  "observation3": "Here's a paradox that has been on my mind lately. If AIs were to improve to equal or surpass human intellect, would they have to be programmed to value human life? And if the question of value is itself closely tied to complexity and to doubt, could AIs ever be complex enough to understand what it means to value human life. This touches on the question of morality, and on the future of artificial consciousness."
